WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.450
Part 4 of nine: Make information accessible and actionable.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.340
The standards connected with this part are 1, 8.

3
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.970
Here is the work this part helps you prepare: Review

4
00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.610
and improve a notice or process instruction

5
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.180
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 8 is described as:

6
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.800
Understandable digital and print materials and signage.

7
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.030
Examine the information itself.

8
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.340
Now that we've looked at assistance, how do we examine

9
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.100
the actual information someone receives?

10
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.050
You have considered the person's experience and the path for

11
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.330
communication assistance. Now take a closer look at the information.

12
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.850
Standard eight addresses understandable digital and print materials and signage

13
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:45.670
in languages used by people served. The principal standard keeps

14
00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.550
the person's communication needs at the center.

15
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.140
We will use five questions: can the person find it,

16
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.900
access it, understand it, use it and act on it?

17
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.240
These questions are a practical review aid. They do not

18
00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.940
replace technical accessibility testing, translation review or applicable agency requirements.

19
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.940
They help you recognize where further work is needed.

20
00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:10.050
Choose a public-facing or non-sensitive example for this activity. If

21
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.610
you are reviewing a real work document, keep it in

22
00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:16.750
the appropriate work location rather than placing personal information in

23
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:17.390
your learning notes.

24
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.540
Find and access.

25
00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.870
Finding the information and being able to access it are

26
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.190
two separate hurdles. What would we look for?

27
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.120
First, can the person find the information? A statement about

28
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.580
assistance may exist somewhere on a website but be absent

29
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.920
from the notice where the person needs it. A link

30
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.260
may use an internal document name that gives no clue

31
00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.040
about its purpose. A telephone option may be difficult to

32
00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.760
locate among several unrelated numbers.

33
00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:46.670
Next, can the person access it? Consider format and method,

34
00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.930
not only whether the file opens on your computer. An

35
00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:53.290
image of text may be unreadable to assistive technology. A

36
00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:56.890
video without accurate captions or a useful transcript may exclude

37
00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:00.570
people. A form that requires a mouse can block keyboard

38
00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.050
users.

39
00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:05.970
A digital-only instruction may be unusable without reliable access to

40
00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:07.430
a suitable device or connection.

41
00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.540
Do not assume you can determine every accessibility issue by

42
00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.540
looking at the screen. Use the established review process and

43
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.180
involve the appropriate accessibility support. If you create documents, use

44
00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.020
meaningful headings, descriptive links, readable structure and the approved tools

45
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.900
for checking the result. Check the document that people will

46
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.240
actually receive, including any exported version.

47
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.530
Understand the message.

48
00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.300
Suppose the person can open the notice. What helps them

49
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.080
understand its meaning?

50
00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.550
Look at our sample notice. It asks the person to

51
00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.770
attend a service-planning meeting, but the opening sentence is filled

52
00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:44.450
with acronyms and internal references. The reader may not know

53
00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:46.470
what the meeting is for, whether they have a choice,

54
00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:48.130
or what they need to do next.

55
00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.200
A clearer structure begins with purpose and action. Explain why

56
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.320
the person is receiving the notice. Identify the next step,

57
00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.440
the relevant date and a way to ask questions or

58
00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.040
request assistance. Explain necessary terms when they first appear. Keep

59
00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.480
essential rights, choices and requirements intact; plain language is not

60
00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.980
permission to remove important meaning.

61
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:15.190
Translation and plain language solve related but different problems. A

62
00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:17.950
translated document can still be difficult to understand if the

63
00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.970
source is unnecessarily complicated. An easy-to-read English version does not

64
00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:25.230
meet every language need. Use the appropriate qualified review and

65
00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:28.550
approved process for the actual audience and purpose. Do not

66
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.610
send confidential information into an unapproved translation service.

67
00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.600
Use and act.

68
00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.430
What makes the difference between understanding a message and being

69
00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:40.230
able to act on it?

70
00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.600
Now ask whether the person can use the information to

71
00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.420
make a decision or complete a step. A notice might

72
00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.800
say to contact the department without naming a usable route.

73
00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.720
A form might explain what is needed but provide no

74
00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.240
way to ask for help. A deadline might be stated

75
00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.960
without explaining what action must be completed by that date.

76
00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:01.570
Make the next step concrete. In our learning example, a

77
00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:04.870
useful notice would explain the meeting's purpose, how to confirm

78
00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:08.210
or discuss the appointment, how to request communication assistance, and

79
00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:11.770
where to ask questions. Use actual approved contact details in

80
00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.990
real work. We are not inventing a new contact or

81
00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:15.710
process here.

82
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.680
Check that the proposed change is within your authority. You

83
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.080
may be able to improve a draft, but a regulated

84
00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.040
notice or official form may require review before use. Preserve

85
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.180
the required content and route the suggested revision to the

86
00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:29.980
person responsible.

87
00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.010
Try a communication review.

88
00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.400
How can we review a draft without claiming that one

89
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:36.900
quick check proves everything works?

90
00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:41.550
In the activity, compare three proposed revisions to the notice.

91
00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.430
Think about what each option would mean for someone who

92
00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.650
needs to understand the information and act on it. Consider

93
00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:51.570
clarity, accessibility, required meaning and a way to request assistance.

94
00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:54.510
Choose the strongest revision, then read the feedback.

95
00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.660
Then apply the five questions to your example. You do

96
00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.580
not have to solve every issue today. Identify one change

97
00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:04.540
you can propose or make, one item that needs specialist

98
00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.320
review, and one way to check whether the revised information

99
00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.140
is more usable.

100
00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:13.090
Where appropriate, feedback from people who use the information can

101
00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:16.650
reveal issues that an internal review misses. That feedback should

102
00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:20.130
be accessible and voluntary, with a clear explanation of how

103
00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.510
it will be used. We will develop that approach in

104
00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:23.730
the community-partnership lesson.

105
00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.980
Carry the change forward.

106
00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.130
What do I carry forward from the communication review?

107
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.580
Save a short description of the improvement in your own

108
00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.460
notes: the barrier, the proposed change, the necessary review and

109
00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:39.600
the expected benefit. Avoid claiming that a document is fully

110
00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.400
accessible simply because it passed one automated check or received

111
00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:44.220
a positive comment.

112
00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:48.510
The goal is a more dependable experience. In the next

113
00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:51.730
lesson, we will connect your proposed improvement to the roles,

114
00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.670
resources and decisions that make it possible to sustain.

115
00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:59.280
Let's work through the companion example: Compare the structure of

116
00:05:59.280 --> 00:05:59.600
a notice

117
00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.610
Here is the situation. Less useful: 'Please attend your annual

118
00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:07.470
review. Contact our office if needed.' The purpose, response task

119
00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:08.990
and assistance route are unclear.

120
00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:12.600
What response would move this example forward responsibly?

121
00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:18.170
Clearer structure: explain why the conversation is happening; state the

122
00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:21.530
date and response step; explain how to discuss an alternative

123
00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:25.450
arrangement or ask questions; include the approved language and disability

124
00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:30.050
assistance information. Use actual authorized wording and contact details in

125
00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:30.670
the real notice.

126
00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:34.100
And here is why that response fits. This is a

127
00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:38.080
learning example, not an official notice. The revision must preserve

128
00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.720
required information and receive the applicable review before use.

129
00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:46.550
Now make your own choice. Which proposed notice revision is

130
00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:46.950
strongest?

131
00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.440
Option 1. Remove technical terms and all required details so

132
00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:53.300
the notice is shorter.

133
00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:58.350
Option 2. Explain the purpose, next step and assistance route

134
00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:02.190
in clear language while preserving required meaning and checking accessibility.

135
00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.460
Option 3. Keep the wording and publish it as a

136
00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.400
picture so its layout stays consistent.

137
00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:12.370
Pause the player now if you want to choose and

138
00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:15.330
explain your reasoning. You can use your own notes or

139
00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.790
the companion activity. When you're ready, resume and we'll work

140
00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:19.930
through all three responses.

141
00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.700
How should we think about option 1?

142
00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:27.910
Shorter is not necessarily clearer or complete. Preserve essential rights,

143
00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.190
choices and requirements, and obtain the required review.

144
00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:33.740
How should we think about option 2?

145
00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:40.610
This addresses understanding and action together. Translation, accessibility and official-content

146
00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:43.390
review may still be needed; one check is not enough.

147
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.080
Option 2 is the supported choice in this activity.

148
00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:49.710
How should we think about option 3?

149
00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:54.560
An image of text may be inaccessible to assistive technology

150
00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.300
and does not address unclear wording. Use an accessible format

151
00:07:58.300 --> 00:07:59.260
and the appropriate checks.

152
00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:02.830
You can choose another response and compare the reasoning.

153
00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:08.300
Can someone find, access, understand, use and act on your

154
00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:12.600
example? Identify one change, one specialist-review need and one way

155
00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:13.220
to check usability.

156
00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:18.190
The working note from this part is: Communication review

157
00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:21.320
Pause the player if you want time to make that

158
00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.380
note. Keep it general, without names or private details. Thinking

159
00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:27.240
it through without writing is also fine.

160
00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:31.250
That completes part 4. You can return to any passage

161
00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:34.210
or take a break. The next part is Connect your

162
00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:36.090
role with organizational responsibility.
